- **AIDLC architecture:** Recommended delivering AIDLC as a plugin rather than a default always-on mode, reducing token consumption and improving the user experience. Advised breaking the AIDLC flow into separate plugins that can each be invoked independently for a small task, or chained to run the full cycle. This positions AIDLC as a flexible framework teams can adopt incrementally, rather than another cumbersome tool.

**Behaviors: The Mastercard Way (HOW)**

**Create Value**

When product proposed creating multiple versions of the specification to replicate Excel-style functionality, I assessed the impact on data growth. Each version would require cloning data across 5 to 6 tables. At roughly 12 versions per publishing cycle and the current rate of 8 publishes a year, that meant about 96 cloned versions annually, with the dataset growing at a compounding rate.

I proposed publishing the data instead, so users can download it without modification. This removed the need for roughly 96 versions a year and simplified the design for end users and engineering alike. The result is a more scalable product with lower storage and maintenance cost.

For AIDLC, I recommended a modular plugin model where each phase can be invoked independently or run as a full cycle. This lowers token cost, improves the experience for developers, and makes adoption easier across teams, which is what allows the framework to scale beyond a single product.

Beyond ModelHub, I contributed reusable AI tooling (PR review skill, usage tracker, AIDLC dashboard) designed to scale across teams rather than serve a single product.

**Move Fast**

- **Prioritize What Matters:** Worked consistently with the product team to establish clearer requirements, and introduced the practice of attaching a priority to every item entering our queue. This lets the team focus on the highest-value work and make deliberate "not now" calls.
- **Prioritize What Matters:** Created a PI monitoring dashboard for two teams to surface spillovers and recurring delays, turning delivery patterns into data the teams can act on rather than discover late.
- **Learn and Pivot:** We pick up higher-priority items first and adjust our approach when priorities shift, without losing track of committed scope.
- **Own the Outcome:** I have owned all 3 releases since joining in July, including the defects that surfaced in them, regardless of when those defects were introduced. I focused on resolving them and putting standards in place to prevent repeats.
